Q1. The fourth zone

The remote repository (such as a repository hosted on GitHub).

Q2. Push vs pull

git push uploads your local commits to a remote repository, while git pull downloads changes from a remote repository and merges them into your current local branch.

Q3. The remote concept

A remote repository is a version of your project hosted on a server or network (like GitHub) that lets you back up code and share changes with others.

Intermediate
Q4. The sync model

Your local repository lives on your computer and tracks commits offline, whereas the remote repository lives on an external server to store a shared and backed-up copy of the project. They stay aligned through explicit sync commands—such as git push, git fetch, and git pull—rather than updating automatically on every file edit.

Q5. The workflow order

Always pull (or fetch and merge) the latest changes from the remote repository first so your local branch is up to date and you can resolve any conflicts before pushing.

Hard
Q6. The visibility problem

Problem 1: Teammates cannot see, review, or collaborate on your code if it only exists on your local laptop.

Problem 2: If your laptop is lost, stolen, or suffers hardware failure, you risk losing your entire project and commit history because there is no off-device backup.

Problem 3: It is difficult to coordinate updates and merge work across multiple devices or contributors without a shared central point of reference.

Q7. Build the four-zone workflow

Working directory to staging: git add . (or git add <file>)

Staging to local repository: git commit -m "Your commit message"

Local repository to remote: git push (or git push -u origin main on the first push)
